there are some things the state cannot  do to direct the moral content of your  life and controlling your sexuality is  one of those things I'm Katherine manga  award for Reason TV and I'm here today  talking with Dale carpenter about his  new book flagrant conduct the story of  Lawrence v Texas thank you for talking  with us thank you I'm glad to be here  just as a kind of background give me a  quick summary of the case then why it  matters  the case was it's a decision of the  Supreme Court that struck down the Texas  homosexual conduct law which forbade  specified sexual activities between two  people of the same sex and in doing so  the Supreme Court said that this  violated the constitutional right to  Liberty which includes a right to adult  intimacy and privacy your book is about  the story behind the story in particular  that the case was presented and and  certainly before I read your book I had  this gauzy idea that it was kind of two  men in a committed relationship who were  you know just like the straight folks  except for unfairly targeted by the law  explain why that narrative was both  useful and false well it was useful  because that sort of narrative was a way  for the court to understand the case  such that it would be acceptable  constitutionally it would be like  protecting people who are in a committed  relationship and the court had come to  have a different understanding I think  of gay people over time because the  justices were more familiar with having  gay clerks and neighbors and friends it  was a false story I think because I  don't believe the police actually saw  anybody having sex and in fact I don't  believe the two men were having sex and  the gay rights attorneys quite  justifiably allowed the case to proceed  on the basis of the allegations of the  police set the scene for us a little bit  you know I think it's hard even in 2012  to really understand how much attitudes  toward homosexuality have evolved from  kind of the stage on which this case  takes place  well the sodomy laws in the United  States had gone back from the founding  and before the founding to the colonies  but they had always covered both  opposite-sex and same-sex activity and  were not just limited to gay people in  the 1970s some states like Texas began  narrowing their laws to focus solely on  on intimacy between two people of the  same sex and making only that criminal  but allowing others to have their own  right to privacy and sexual autonomy  that came out I think of an atmosphere  of real stigma attached to gay men and  lesbians all across the country but  especially in places like Texas and it  came out and the fact that the  legislature refused to repeal the law  even though it was asked to do so in  every session courts refused to strike  it down because people were not actually  getting arrested and the law was used to  brand gay men and lesbians as criminals  and therefore as a basis to deny them  jobs housing you name it across the  board you tell a great little story  about right as the oral arguments are  about to take place a sort of tip that  the the lawyer gets about Sandra Day  O'Connor can you retell that story for  us the attorney for Lambda Legal which  was the National gay rights group that  led the challenge against the Texas  sodomy law was about to get up and  deliver his oral argument to the  justices back in March 2003 and it's a  high pressure moment for anyone but  especially for this man in this case and  just before he gets up to deliver his  argument someone whispers into his ear  that justice O'Connor had just sent a  former clerk of hers and her lesbian  partner a baby shower gift since they  had had a baby that really encapsulate  the changes that occurred had occurred  on the court by the year 2003 when all  of the justices news  who was gay in contrast to 17 years  before when the court had upheld sodomy  laws in a case called Bowers versus  Hardwick and one of the justices told  his clerk that he had never met someone  who was gay even though that clerk turns  out to him himself had been a closeted  gay person so the case is just full of  ironies and  little twists throughout talk a little  bit about why this case is about more  than just gay right well in in part it's  about more than that because it revives  a constitutional doctrine that protects  a right to liberty and privacy and  sexual autonomy for adults it's a  limitation on the power of government to  intrude on our personal lives it says  some there are some things the state  cannot do to direct the moral content of  your life and control in your sexuality  is one of those things can you talk a  little bit about the kind of stagecraft  of these Supreme Court cases I mean what  what struck me was just how incredibly  powerful the the lawyers the attorneys  who kind of shaped the case shaped the  public's perception of the case how  powerful they are in rewriting the life  stories of these men I mean Lawrence had  a long term boyfriend who was not  involved in this case at all and this  person sort of erased from the story  among among other people you know is  your is your kind of sense that that's  very common and that much of what we  hear and see coming out of the court is  this narrowed kind of narrative or was  there something special about this case  well I think both of those things are  true actually it's it's common for the  court only to get a very distilled  essence of the facts which it then  reviews to determine things like  constitutionality the court is not does  not often get into the weeds of the  facts of a case and in this case the  court was not encouraged to do so in  part because there was no trial of the  police made a charge they arrested the  two men dragged him out of their home  took them off to jail find them and did  so on the basis what I believe were  false charges but the gay rights  attorneys had no interest in challenging  the factual allegations made by the  state so everyone's interest was in  seeing this version of the case proceed  through the courts the police not  wanting to admit that it wasn't true gay  rights attorneys wanting a challenge  involving the application of such a law  in a private space and our legal system  it turns out our criminal justice system  is not a search for truth it is not an  inquisitorial system we don't have a  Inquisitor who demands answers from  criminal defendants instead we have an  adversarial system and sometimes that  adversarial system works to ferret out  the truth and sometimes that edvard  adversarial system assumes a kind of  alternate truth that then becomes the  basis for a larger decision so the court  is essentially almost deciding a  hypothetical but but it doesn't matter  because this is the question that people  want the court to answer this is what  the court is for right I would say you  could certainly describe it as a  hypothetical although in this case  because the men were actually arrested  that arrest was not hypothetical and the  charges that were made against them were  not hypothetical and the problem that  the litigants had was that the police  weren't using this law to arrest people  in the privacy of their home and judges  were requiring that that happened before  you could challenge the law and yet and  yet the law was having an effect  throughout society so it was you in a  sense had to get around that technical  requirement of having to get arrested to  get at the meat of the law I don't think  the attorneys ever misrepresented  anything the police may have represented  something in the facts of the case and  the attorneys allow that to proceed and  took full advantage of it thank you very  much Dale carpenter thank you for reason  TV I'm Katherine Maggie ward
 

